<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Augsburg Confession</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1530">1530</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Confessio Augustana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> Confession is the founding statement of Lutheran belief, drafted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Philip Melanchthon</a> and presented to Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> at the Diet of Augsburg in 1530. It was signed by several princes, including Wolfgang of Anhalt, and by the imperial cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> and Reutlingen, and other cities soon subscribed. It became the legal basis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-augsburg">Peace of Augsburg</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/augsburg-confession.jpg" alt="Augsburg Confession" width="320" height="263" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Augsburger-Reichstag.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1530 the Confutatio Augustana answered the Lutheran <b>Augsburg Confession</b>, and in 1540 the Jesuits were canonically recognised.</blockquote><a href="/movement/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>New texts gave the churches their shape: Luther’s German Bible and his Small Catechism of 1529, the <b>Augsburg Confession</b> drawn up by Philip Melanchthon in 1530, Thomas Cranmer’s Book of Common Prayer of 1549 and Reformed statements of faith such as the Heidelberg Catechism of 1563.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Diet of Augsburg, German rulers and free cities presented Emperor Charles V with the <b>Augsburg Confession</b>, prepared by Philipp Melanchthon at the behest of John, Elector of Saxony.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-25">25 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Augsburg Confession</b> was presented at the diet of 1530, and the Peace of Augsburg of 1555 recognised Lutheranism.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-augsburg">Free Imperial City of Augsburg</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the preacher Matthäus Alber it adopted the Reformation in the 1520s, and in 1530 it was one of only two cities to sign the <b>Augsburg Confession</b>, with Nuremberg.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-reutlingen">Free Imperial City of Reutlingen</a></li><li><blockquote>Windsheim adopted the Reformation in the 1520s and subscribed to the <b>Augsburg Confession</b> soon after it was presented in 1530.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-windsheim">Free Imperial City of Windsheim</a></li></ul></section>
